  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Jos\u00e9 Caballero's home run in the third gave them air they didn't have to give back.",
    "what_this_means": "Cole bought them six innings of one-run ball. Tight, controlled, exactly what you need in a one-run game. The offense found two runs early and then spent the rest of the night looking for a third. Nine strikeouts against five hits is not a formula that wins often. Headrick, Blackburn, and Bednar closed it out clean, three pitchers throwing 2.4 innings of nothing. The bullpen held. That matters. But this game sat on the edge of the third inning for seven innings after that. Two runners on base in the fourth with a chance to break it open. They got nothing. Two more in the seventh. Nothing again. They won by a run for the third time in five games. The math gets harder when you keep leaving runners stranded in spots where one swing changes the shape of the game.",
    "what_to_watch": "McGreevy is 3.57 on the season. Right in their wheelhouse if the lineup shows patience instead of chasing early.",
